content.text:
Now reload the mobile game to pick up changes, start, then simulate stick forward + web hold.

First reload m2, start, then eval:
```
(async()=>{
 const stick=document.getElementById('stick'); const r=stick.getBoundingClientRect();
 stick.dispatchEvent(new PointerEvent('pointerdown',{pointerId:11,pointerType:'touch',bubbles:true,clientX:r.left+r.width/2, clientY:r.top+r.height/2 - 40}));
 const web=document.getElementById('webBtn');
 web.dispatchEvent(new PointerEvent('pointerdown',{pointerId:12,pointerType:'touch',bubbles:true}));
 await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1200));
 return {info:window.__game.info, stick:...}
})()
```
But the eval wrapper might not await the async function result? Playwright's evaluate awaits promises. Should work.

Then screenshot. Then pointerup.

Let's reload first.

Menu now shows the city behind, and the character is partially visible behind the controls (a red head at the center). The view is decent but the character is obscured by the controls panel. The overlay dims. The title is huge and good. The background could be a more impressive angle (rooftop overview) instead of a street canyon behind a translucent panel. The menu camera drifts around the spawn (street). We could set the menu camera to a cinematic orbit above the city showing skyscrapers, not behind the character. That would sell the game better.

Idea: In menu, position the camera high above the city orbiting a point near the center with a sweeping view, showing the skyline at sunset. The player can be visible on the street? Not necessary. The start screen background should be a beautiful city vista. But the overlay is translucent; a rooftop view would look great.

Currently in menu loop:
```
camYaw = sin(time*0.09)*0.35;
camPitch = 0.2 + sin(time*0.13)*0.04;
```
and updateCamera follows the player (at spawn street), so it's a low street-level orbit. We can override the menu camera separately:
```
if (state.phase === 'menu') {
  const a = time * 0.06;
  camera.position.set(Math.cos(a)*85, 55 + Math.sin(time*0.1)*8, 95 + Math.sin(a)*85);
  camera.lookAt(0, 30, 30);
  camera.fov = 58;
  ...
}
```
But updateCamera is called every frame and would override. Add a branch in updateCamera: if phase==='menu', run cinematic camera and return (after sky follow, etc.). Need the sky to follow camera too. Or set a flag.

Let's design a menu camera: orbit around the city center at radius ~150, height 70, looking at the center (0, 45, 0). The center has the landmark tower. This will show a skyline with sun. But the player is at the street spawn, not visible. That's fine for a title screen.

Menu camera:
```
const a = time*0.045;
const radius=175;
camera.position.set(Math.cos(a)*radius, 60 + Math.sin(time*0.12)*7, Math.sin(a)*radius);
camera.lookAt(0, 40, 0);
camera.fov=60; updateProjectionMatrix if changed
sky.position.copy(camera.position);
sun shadow position maybe not relevant
```
But this is a fixed orbit; the city center tower visible. Better angle: camera at radius 210 beyond the edge, height 85, looking at (0, 40, 0). Orbit slow. The start overlay covers the center though; the title is over the city. Fine.

At menu start (0s) camera is at (175, 60, 0), looking at center; the landmark tower is at center, good.

When starting the game, updateCamera smoothly lerps from this distant camera to the player camera. It could fly in dramatically. Since camera.position.lerp with rate 14, it will swoop from 175 units away to behind the player in ~0.5s. That's a nice transition, though it may clip through buildings. Acceptable. Or snap camera on start. A swoop is cool.

Need to ensure the menu camera doesn't use camYaw/camPitch (which are modified). In updateCamera:
```
if (state.phase === 'menu') {
  const a = time*0.05;
  ...
  camera.position.set(...)
  camera.lookAt(0,38,0)
  camera.fov = damp(camera.fov, 60, 4, dt); update
  sky.position.copy(camera.position)
  sun...
  return;
}
```
But the main loop also updates `camYaw` in menu to sin... Remove that block or leave; it doesn't matter if the menu camera branch ignores it. On start, camYaw=0 (set in startGame? not reset). We set camYaw in menu block; would carry into gameplay, altering the start view. Remove the menu camYaw updates so camYaw stays 0.

Camera transition on start: from orbit to gameplay. `camera.position.lerp(desired, 1-exp(-14dt))` each frame; the first frame moves 20% of the distance (35 units), potentially through buildings. Over 0.5s. The menu overlay hides it. Fine.

Let's implement. Also, the character is not visible in menu; that's okay. The HUD is hidden. The title overlay's center is the city. The background will be better.

Menu camera radius 200, height 78, look at (0,35,0). At angle 0, camera at (200,78,0) looking west toward center. The sun is at (110,145,120) direction; sky sun glow visible? Depends. The city center might be far. With fog end 590, visible. Buildings will be seen from above, which is the best angle. 

Let's implement and screenshot.

Also the overlay background at 0.7/0.42 dims the city; maybe lighten further to 0.55/0.35 and blur 1px. The screenshot's background currently looks nice and legible. With a city vista, lighten a bit.

Let's do menu camera.

